former Supreme Court Justice John Paul  Stevens is being remembered for his  towering judicial career Stevens among  the longest-serving justices in court  history died yesterday after  complications from a stroke he was 99  years old and served on the court for 35  years Jan Crawford reports on his legacy  on the bench John Paul Stevens was known  for his fierce independence  old-fashioned geniality and that  trademark bowtie he gradually  transformed from a moderate conservative  to a leading liberal voice I don't  really think I've changed I think there  have been a lot of changes in the court  I am confident that he will bring both  professional and personal qualities of  the highest order  President Gerald Ford 1975 appointed  Stevens to the court I will do  everything in my power to render the  best possible judicial service of which  I am capable he would go on to serve  under seven presidents his decisions in  part helped promote racial equality gay  rights and uphold legal abortions but  his biggest decision came in 2000 with  the court's decision to stop the Florida  presidential vote recount in Bush versus  Gore he told 60 minutes ten years later  it was one of the courts biggest  blunders I don't question the good faith  of the people on the the justices with  whom I disagreed but I think they were  profoundly wrong Stephens retired in  2010 he has stood as an impartial  guardian of the law in his retirement he  gave opinions from the sidelines calling  for repealing the Second Amendment and  abolishing the death penalty this is a  country in which people can disagree  without being disagreeable but try not  to be disagreeable in  opinion writing I think for the most  part I succeed  now Chief Justice John Roberts said in a  statement that Stevens his unrelenting  commitment to justice has left us a  better nation he's expected to be buried  in Arlington National Cemetery next to  his wife Mary Ann Gayle all right Thank  You Jan we all need more of that  disagree without being disagreeable with  us let's remember that
 

